Sure, here's a formal documentation of the testing approach for the Invoice Management REST service with the given tools: Gauge, Datafaker, AssertJ, and Oracle EBS integration.

**Overview**

This testing approach uses Gauge as the BDD test framework, Datafaker to generate fake data, AssertJ for standard assertions, and integrates with Oracle EBS to test creation of invoices based on REST API calls. The following steps outline the testing process:

1. Define the test scenarios in Gauge specification.
2. Generate fake data using Datafaker for input parameters and expected outcomes.
3. Use Spring Boot's Dependency Injection to inject dependencies and interact with the tested REST API.
4. Make HTTP POST requests to the REST API endpoint (/invoices) and inspect response data.
5. Verify response data assertions using AssertJ.

**Prerequisites**

- Gauge installed and configured.
- Datafaker and appropriate language bindings installed and configured.
- Spring Boot project with Oracle EBS integration.
- Familiarity with Gauge BDD, Datafaker, AssertJ, and Spring Boot frameworks.

**Testing Approach - Steps**

1. Create a Gauge specification file (gspec) with the following structure:

   ```gauge
   Description: Invoice Management REST service tests

   Scenario: Create a new invoice
     Given the following input data:
       | invoiceNumber | customerName   | orderTotal   |
       | 1234          | Bob's Supplies | 100.50       |
     When I make a POST request to "/invoices"
     Then the response should have status code 200
          And the response body should contain:
          | invoiceNumber | customerName    | orderTotal   |
          | 1234          | Bob's Supplies | 100.50       |
          | invocationId | <unique-id>   |             |
   ```

2. Add Datafaker configuration and generation for input data and expected outcomes. The sample input data and expected outcome can be generated as follows:

   ```gauge
   Feature: Invoice Management REST service tests

   Background:
     Given Datafaker configuration:
       | fakerLanguage   | fakerVersion |
       | English         | 1.11.2       |

   Scenario: Create a new invoice
     Given I generate the following fake input data using Datafaker:
       | Field               | Value                      |
       | invoiceNumber       | {{unique.random_digit_n(6, 4)}} |
       | customerName        | {{name.lastName}} {{name.firstName}}      |
       | orderTotal          | {{random.float(10, 1000, 2)}}  |
     When I make a POST request to "/invoices"
     Then the response should have status code 200
          And the response body should contain:
          | Field                 | Value                      |
          | invoiceNumber         | {{scenario.variable("invoiceNumber")}} |
          | customerName          | {{scenario.variable("customerName")}}    |
          | orderTotal            | {{scenario.variable("orderTotal")}}      |
          | invocationId          | <unique-id>                   |
   ```

3. Use Spring Boot's Dependency Injection to inject the dependency handling HTTP requests. Add a step to your Gauge implementation code to interact with the REST API. For example, in Java:

   ```java
   import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
   import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
   import org.springframework.boot.test.web.client.TestRestTemplate;
   import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
   import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
   import org.springframework.test.context.ContextConfiguration;

   @ContextConfiguration(classes = MySpringBootApp.class)
  @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
   public class InvoiceManagementTest {

       @Autowired
       private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

       // rest client interaction methods here

   }
   ```

4. Add a method to a Gauge step implementation file that sends an HTTP POST request to the "/invoices" endpoint and returns the response:

   ```java
   import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.post;
   import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.status;

   public class StepImplementation extends AbstractGaugeTest {

      private TestRestTemplate testRestTemplate;

      @Override
      public void setup() {
         this.testRestTemplate = new MySpringBootTestTestRestTemplate();
      }

      public void createInvoice(String invoiceNumber, String customerName, String orderTotal) {
         String uri = "/invoices";
         String requestContent =
                 "{ \"invoiceNumber\": \"" + invoiceNumber + "\", " +
                         "\"customerName\": \"" + customerName + "\", " +
                         "\"orderTotal\": \"" + orderTotal + "\" }";
         this.testRestTemplate.postForObject(uri, requestContent, Void.class);
         // Additional response assertion with AssertJ
         this.testRestTemplate.perform(post(uri)
                 .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
                 .content(requestContent))
                 .andExpect(status().isOk());
      }
   }
   ```

5. Add an assertion method using AssertJ to compare the response data against the expected outcome. For example:

   ```java
   import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

   public class StepImplementation extends AbstractGaugeTest {

      // ...

      @Then("the response body should contain:")
      public void validateResponse(String[][] dataTable) {
         ResponseEntity<?> responseEntity = this.testRestTemplate.getForEntity("/invoices", Object.class);

         assertThat(responseEntity.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
         assertThat(responseEntity.getBody()).isNotNull();

         Invoice invoice = (Invoice) responseEntity.getBody();
         assertThat(invoice.getInvoiceNumber()).isEqualTo(dataTable[0][0]);
         assertThat(invoice.getCustomerName()).isEqualTo(dataTable[0][1]);
         assertThat(invoice.getOrderTotal()).isEqualTo(Double.parseDouble(dataTable[0][2]));
         assertThat(invoice.getInvocationId()).isNotEqualTo(dataTable[0][3]); // unique-id expected to be different
      }

      // ...
   }
   ```

**Conclusion**

By following the above approach, you can perform end-to-end functional testing of the Invoice Management REST service using Gauge, Datafaker, AssertJ, and Oracle EBS integration. This approach generates fake data, injects dependencies, engages with a REST API, and verifies response data using AssertJ. With this comprehensive test coverage, you can ensure the quality of the Invoice Management service.

